      can glimpse the sky doubled and tripled in endless succession, a sight that scholars of Quelm have spent centuries trying to explain.
  13. The Hollowheart Yew is so old that its inner trunk has become a vast chamber lined with soft moss and dripping with dew. Hermits have lived inside its cavity for generations,
      and they carve tiny windows into the wood to watch the seasons pass through the gaps in the ancient bark.
  14. Gleamroot Mangroves grow along the tidal flats of Caldera Bay, and their exposed roots glow a faint green when disturbed by footsteps. Fishermen use the glow to find the safest
      paths through the mud, though the roots bruise easily and must never be walked upon without care.
  15. The Whispering Larch of the Duskmarch stands alone on a hill and speaks in a language of rustling needles. Scholars who learned the cadence believe the larch recounts the
      history of the hill, naming every ruler who ever stood beneath it, and some say it still mourns a king who never returned.
  16. Pearlbloom Magnolias blossom only once every hundred years, and each flower opens to reveal a small, perfectly formed pearl at its center. The town of Lunevale holds a festival
      whenever the trees bloom, and the pearls are carefully gathered and distributed among the families who tended the grove.
  17. Stormcrown Firs grow on the windward peaks of the Thundrel Range and draw lightning into their branches like a lightning rod. Their needles store the electrical charge for days,
      and climbers who touch them during storms feel a faint tingle that lingers in their fingertips long after the clouds have passed.
  18. Silkweave Banyans send down aerial roots that weave into intricate tapestries, each pattern unique to the tree and recording the events that happened nearby. Weavers of the
      Mirrani coast read these tapestries as chronicles, and a single banyan can hold a thousand years of local history woven into its hanging veils.
  19. The Mossgarden Redwood grows an entire ecosystem on its upper branches, with soil, ferns, small lakes, and a community of mole-like creatures that never descend to the ground.
      Its canopy is so dense that rain falls on the forest floor only in rare, heavy storms, and the moss up high is said to hold the dreams of the creatures who live there.
  20. Fogbrush Junipers cling to the cliffs above the Orrin Sea and exhale a thick mist every evening. The mist drifts down to the harbor and cloaks the fishing fleet in softness, and
      sailors have learned to steer by the particular scent of juniper that rides within the fog.
  21. Chimeron Sycamores bear fruit that changes flavor depending on the mood of the person who picks it. Bitter when the picker is angry, sweet when they are content, and faintly
      sour when they are uncertain, the sycamores of the Ammerin valley have been studied by generations of confectioners who never quite agree on the cause.
  22. The Frostveil Spruce grows a shimmering coat of ice crystals that never melts, even in summer. Mountain shepherds wrap their flocks in the fallen crystals during heat waves, and
      the crystals keep the animals cool for days before finally dissolving into a fine, sweet dew.
  23. Ashbloom Acacias thrive in the volcanic plains of Morrel, where their flowers grow from ash and smoke. The blossoms release a faint perfume that calms restless animals, and
      herders plant acacias around their camps to keep the livestock settled through the long, windy nights.
  24. Lanternroot Figs drape their branches with hanging fruit that glows softly after dusk. Travelers on the old salt road use the fig groves as natural beacons, and the innkeepers
      of Port Varek keep the trees carefully pruned so that the light falls in a steady line along the road.
  25. The Riddlebark Cypress carves its own message into its trunk each year, always written in a script that no one has yet decoded. Linguists from three continents have proposed
      competing translations, and the cypress seems to find their efforts amusing, shedding bark in patterns that suggest a grin.
  26. Dawnseed Hazels bloom before the sun rises, releasing clouds of golden pollen that drift upward and catch the first light. The effect transforms the hazel groves of Eldrenne
      into a field of tiny, drifting stars for about an hour, after which the pollen settles and the hazels quietly return to their ordinary green.
  27. Tidewhisper Palms grow along the coastline of Sallow Isle, and their fronds sway in rhythm with the tide. Local sailors believe the palms can predict the weather by the speed of
      their movement, and they consult the fronds before deciding whether to set out on a long crossing.
  28. The Cloudhollow Ash has a trunk so pale it appears almost translucent in morning light. Its branches are hollow and fill with drifting fog, and in the early hours the ash seems
      to float above the valley, untethered from the ground beneath it.
  29. Sunlattice Olives grow in terraced hillsides above the Varenna basin, and their leaves turn to face the sun throughout the day like patient sunflowers. The olives produce oil
      that stores light and releases it slowly during dark evenings, so families who own these groves rarely need to light lamps.
  30. Hushroot Alders hold the riverbanks of the Lethe Valley and absorb the noise of the flowing water into their roots. The valley is famously quiet, and visitors often remark on
      how the river seems to flow without sound, a peace that the alders maintain with stubborn persistence.
  31. The Bellwether Tamarind rings a soft chime whenever the seasons change. Farmers time their planting by its song, and a particularly old specimen in the Tivoli orchards is said
      to have announced the coming of three droughts and two floods with remarkable accuracy.
  32.

